WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.584
- I wasn't highly recruited.

00:00:01.584 --> 00:00:03.128
I wanted to go to Maryland.

00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:04.796
And the Maryland coach
was like, "Ah, you

00:00:04.796 --> 00:00:05.547
gotta work on your jump shot."

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:07.257
- I started playing
in fourth grade.

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:08.508
I, too, was not good.

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.844
I was tripping over
lines on the floor.

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:11.886
- [Laughing]

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:13.596
- What have you thought
about finances?

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:15.557
- One of our owners, they
own the stock exchange!

00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:17.600
So it's like, why am I
not talking about stocks

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.643
and what I can get into?

00:00:18.643 --> 00:00:20.770
- It would never occur to
me to do that, because, you know,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:21.855
you come into the league at 22.

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.815
I'm like, "I'm gonna
buy an apartment complex?"

00:00:23.815 --> 00:00:25.692
- [Laughing]

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:28.862
- This is "The Reunion," where
former college athletes meet up

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:32.365
to discuss lessons learned
about sports, money and life.

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:39.998
I'm Taylor Rooks, here
with Angel McCoughtry

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:41.041
and Napheesa Collier.

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:43.626
Well, you know, before we really
dive into things,

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:46.004
I want us to zoom out a bit.

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:49.507
You know, so much time has passed. You know,
you guys being in the WNBA,

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:50.967
time in college.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:52.969
When you look at this
moment right now,

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:56.931
did you envision your life
being what it is right now?

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:57.640
- I didn't.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.893
Even growing up, playing
with all the boys,

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:02.437
they're like, "Hey, you can play
in this thing called the WNBA

00:01:02.437 --> 00:01:02.687
one day."

00:01:02.687 --> 00:01:04.022
And I'm like, "What's that?"
You know?

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:06.399
And then I started to envision
playing in the WNBA.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.735
But I never envisioned going
to different countries,

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.529
learning different
languages, being an Olympian,

00:01:11.529 --> 00:01:12.947
getting a gold medal, right?

00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:17.744
So those are experiences
that you just—I mean, people dream of, you know?
- Mmhmm.

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.287
- So it's been an amazing ride.

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:20.872
- I think it was
the dream, but you

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:22.040
don't know what to envision

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.875
so you see the big
milestones. Like, I wanna

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:25.126
go to the Olympics someday.
- Right.

00:01:25.126 --> 00:01:27.045
- I wanna play in
the WNBA someday.

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:29.547
But it's like the day-to-day—like,
this kind of stuff

00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:32.050
or, like, playing overseas—that you
really don't think about.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.677
I didn't, at least, in high
school and college, really.

00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:37.430
- I mean, it's so cool
to think, you know, just how

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.766
beautiful it is
that basketball now

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:41.184
has given you so many things.

00:01:41.184 --> 00:01:42.894
And the work that you
put into basketball

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:44.270
has given you so many things.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.815
So I wanna know when you knew
you were better than everyone

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:47.315
else?

00:01:47.315 --> 00:01:48.274
- [Laughing]

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.360
- When did y'all
know you were good?

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:51.194
- You know what?

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:54.030
OK, so I started at eight
years old. Always played,

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:54.948
just loved it.

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:57.367
And I was, like, the last
one coming off the bench.

00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:59.035
I was not that player.

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:00.787
People would think
that from our success,

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:01.913
but I wasn't that player.

00:02:01.913 --> 00:02:03.331
I feel like during
my career, I've

00:02:03.331 --> 00:02:05.500
always had to be the type
to prove people wrong.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.418
I wasn't highly recruited.

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:09.963
I wanted to go to Maryland,
and the Maryland coach

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:10.505
was like, "Ah,

00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:11.923
you gotta work
on your jump shot."

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:13.883
So I've always had to
overcome these obstacles.

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:16.594
And even at Louisville, them
not going past the first round

00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:17.345
of the tournament.

00:02:17.345 --> 00:02:19.973
But by my senior year, we're in
the national championship game,

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:21.766
playing UConn, being ranked.

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:23.643
So I've always had to overcome.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.645
To be a part of a
legacy with Louisville,

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.063
it's really, really good to see that.

00:02:27.063 --> 00:02:28.690
Cuz now, more girls are
going to different schools

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.191
and creating their own legacies.
- Mmhmm.

00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:32.527
- Yeah, I started playing
in fourth grade.

00:02:32.527 --> 00:02:33.862
- Mmhmm.
- So kinda around the same time.

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:35.155
I, too, was not good.

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:36.531
I was super tall, lanky.

00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:39.242
I've always been, like, tripping
over lines on the floor.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:40.660
- [Laughing]

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.744
- Not graceful at all.

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:44.831
I think I started noticing that
I was getting better probably

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.750
around, like, sixth grade,
seventh grade, and then

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:48.710
eighth grade for sure.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.795
You kinda see the
separation a little bit there.

00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:51.254
- [Angel:] Start to see it, yeah.

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:53.506
- [Taylor:] I think we should take a
lil' trip down memory lane.
- [Angel:] Yes.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:56.593
- I know how crazy, how loud
it would get in Storrs.

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:59.971
How would you just describe
that fanbase at UConn?

00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:01.472
- You know, our gym was a
little bit smaller,

00:03:01.472 --> 00:03:03.016
so it only fit 10,000 people.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.267
So it felt so loud.

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:05.602
But our fans are so cool.

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:07.979
And like I said, some of my
best memories are from there,

00:03:07.979 --> 00:03:10.481
and a lot of it had to do
with the games and the fans.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:12.984
- So, when you all
make it to the WNBA

00:03:12.984 --> 00:03:15.069
and you're having these
conversations about money,

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:17.113
making decisions,
who did you go to?

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:18.823
- I'm still learning, I feel like.

00:03:18.823 --> 00:03:22.160
Because—you know what it is? Even though,
like you said,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.204
our paychecks aren't a
lot, it's still more money

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:24.913
than I've ever seen.

00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:26.581
So it feels like you
have, like, so much money

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:27.415
when you first start out.

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:27.999
- Yeah.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:29.751
- So relying on people
like my agent,

00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:31.920
my parents, you know, the older
people on my team

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.087
who have been through this.

00:03:33.087 --> 00:03:35.048
- I think you make a few
mistakes before you realize,

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.549
"OK, this is the
route I need to go."

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:38.301
I think any successful
businessperson

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:40.386
will tell you that they've
made so many mistakes before.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.804
And it's nothing
to be ashamed of.

00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:43.348
It's a learning experience.

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:44.390
You know, you wanna be able
to call up people

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.642
you know for different things.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:48.811
So even to encourage all the
players, talk to your owners.

00:03:48.811 --> 00:03:52.148
Your owners are big-time
successful businesspeople.

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:53.399
Ask them their story.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:54.609
Talk to them about
what they're doing.

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:55.860
I didn't know that
in the beginning.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:56.486
- Yeah.
- You know?

00:03:56.486 --> 00:03:59.113
So that's just a little word of
advice for everyone out there.

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:01.783
- For someone watching who
maybe doesn't understand

00:04:01.783 --> 00:04:03.993
WNBA players going
overseas, can you

00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:05.370
explain that process for them?

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:05.870
- Yes.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.330
It is a long grind.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.708
You're talking about eight
months in another country.

00:04:10.708 --> 00:04:13.002
Great thing is your family
becomes your teammates.

00:04:13.002 --> 00:04:14.212
It is very tough.

00:04:14.212 --> 00:04:15.546
You have to be mentally tough.

00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:17.048
- My first year was 2020.

00:04:17.048 --> 00:04:18.299
I was going to play in China.

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:19.133
I was there for 10 days.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.636
And then coronavirus hit there
first, so I had to come home.

00:04:21.636 --> 00:04:23.680
I took, like, a half-year
playing in France, like,

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.056
going into the WNBA season

00:04:25.056 --> 00:04:26.641
so that my body would be ready
and things like that,

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:27.809
but I could also enjoy my time.

00:04:27.809 --> 00:04:28.726
- But that's still a sacrifice.
- Yeah.

00:04:28.726 --> 00:04:30.979
- Just the fact that you go over
there, that's a sacrifice.

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:31.521
- Yeah!

00:04:31.521 --> 00:04:33.064
Yeah, absolutely.
- [Angel:] Yes!

00:04:33.064 --> 00:04:35.733
- Yeah, and you're just thinking,
"OK, well, when can I see them?"

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:37.902
But then in the back of
your head, you're also like,

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:40.863
"I'm doing this for them as well."

00:04:40.863 --> 00:04:43.741
- You know, before she retires, I want them
to have their first million-dollar

00:04:43.741 --> 00:04:45.159
contracts.

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:48.162
They deserve it, and I feel
like it is coming soon.

00:04:48.162 --> 00:04:49.622
I would not be mad about that.

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:50.498
- [Laughing]

00:04:50.498 --> 00:04:52.583
- [Napheesa:] I retweet that.

00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:55.461
- [Taylor:] So what has, or how has your definition
of a good teammate

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.046
evolved as you've gotten older?

00:04:57.046 --> 00:04:59.882
- Having good communication, being
supportive, being encouraging

00:04:59.882 --> 00:05:01.301
and being a little
bit vulnerable.

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.052
Your teammates wanna
see you be vulnerable.

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:04.554
I think as I've
gotten more vulnerable,

00:05:04.554 --> 00:05:06.222
I've gotten close
with my teammates.

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:07.849
- Something I do always
wanna frame—

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:09.434
and I want you all's
take on this—

00:05:09.434 --> 00:05:12.186
I think too often when
we're talking to girls,

00:05:12.186 --> 00:05:16.232
young women, we frame being a
woman, like, as an obstacle, something

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:17.859
you're having to
overcome all the time.

00:05:17.859 --> 00:05:20.069
And I don't think that that's
the right way to frame it.

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:23.865
I know we all gain a lot of
strength, you know, from being women.

00:05:23.865 --> 00:05:27.160
In what ways, you know, has just
your experience as a woman

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.787
made you a stronger,
better person?

00:05:29.787 --> 00:05:30.830
- You have to be stern.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:31.873
You have to be confident.

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:34.876
You have to know what you want,
so your no's aren't negotiated.

00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:36.753
- Yeah, "no" is a complete sentence.

00:05:36.753 --> 00:05:38.171
That's what I'm always told.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.297
"No" is a complete sentence.

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:40.089
What about for you?

00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:43.426
- I think because people
see it as an obstacle,

00:05:43.426 --> 00:05:44.427
it makes us tougher.

00:05:44.427 --> 00:05:46.346
Being a woman is
so, so powerful.

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:49.265
And we have, like, this inner
strength because even

00:05:49.265 --> 00:05:51.601
if people are doubting us,
we know that we can do it.

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:52.143
- [Angel:] Exactly.

00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:53.436
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:53.436 --> 00:05:57.482
So, as you look forward
to the next 10 years,

00:05:57.482 --> 00:05:58.775
what do you wanna accomplish?

00:05:58.775 --> 00:06:00.985
- I probably wanna be in
the position that you're in,

00:06:00.985 --> 00:06:02.695
probably the exact
same one, you know?

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:06.491
Getting ready to wind down my career
and financially stable.

00:06:06.491 --> 00:06:08.701
And, like, you're so
knowledgeable about it.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.912
I feel like there's so
much I still need to learn.

00:06:10.912 --> 00:06:13.331
So I wanna be in a position
where I know what I'm doing,

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:15.750
I can go confidently
into new investments,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.127
know how to research
them, things like that.

00:06:18.127 --> 00:06:21.464
So really just kind of learning
all the stuff that I don't even

00:06:21.464 --> 00:06:22.215
know I don't know.

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:23.216
- Yeah.
- Awesome, yeah.

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:24.717
- Next 10 years for you.

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.011
- I wanna be a
great businesswoman.

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:29.722
I wanna be able to
also help the younger

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:31.557
generation, the younger
girls in the league.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:33.601
And I wanna be able to
help the young girls who

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:38.481
are kinda green to not knowing, you know?
Cuz I, we didn't know, you know?
- Yeah.

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:40.608
- And be just a great businesswoman.

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:45.321
I would love to be like the
LeBron James of, you know, the WNBA.

00:06:45.321 --> 00:06:46.739
With everything
he's doing, I just

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.449
wanna have my
hands in everything.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:49.158
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:49.158 --> 00:06:52.203
Well, I am so inspired
by both of you.

00:06:52.203 --> 00:06:54.539
I'm so happy to be sitting
here with you guys.

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:55.665
- Thank you so much.

